Field Type Default Meaning <code>success_threshold</code> <code>float</code> <code>1.0</code> The success threshold, inclusive. <code>direction</code> <code>MetricDirection</code> or <code>"hib"</code> / <code>"lib"</code> <code>"hib"</code> Higher is better (<code>value &gt;= threshold</code> succeeds) or lower is better (<code>value &lt;= threshold</code>). <code>metric_mode</code> <code>MetricMode</code> or <code>"interval"</code> / <code>"reset"</code> <code>"interval"</code> When success is judged: every step, or once on the episode's value at reset. <code>is_cumulative</code> <code>bool</code> <code>False</code> Sum the value over the episode. Success checks and logs use the sum divided by the episode length (the per-step mean). <code>contributes_to_success</code> <code>bool</code> <code>True</code> Whether the term counts toward the task's success at all. <p>The metric manager reads these five once, when it is built; a later write to one (from a curriculum, say) has no effect. Set them through <code>configure()</code>.</p> <p>A function term can carry its own defaults for them at registration, so every use of it starts right:</p> <pre><code>from eden.constants import MetricDirection, MetricMode
from eden.managers import METRIC_TERM_REGISTRY


@METRIC_TERM_REGISTRY.register(is_cumulative=True, direction=MetricDirection.LIB, metric_mode=MetricMode.RESET)
def error_vel_xy(env, *, command_name: str = "velocity"):
    ...
</code></pre> <p>That is how <code>error_vel_xy</code>, <code>error_vel_yaw</code> (<code>eden/managers/terms/metrics/error.py</code>) and the distance metrics (<code>entity_distance</code>, <code>entity_distance_xy</code>: lower is better) are registered. A value passed to <code>configure()</code> still wins.</p>
